Keep the game running while the app is in the background and its sound plays.

Switching to another app (a browser, Steam Guard in the Steam Mobile app) suspended the app at
once, and with it the game's connection to its servers, and the Steam session the game uses.
The audio adapters already hold an AVAudioSession in the playback category while the game's
sound runs (translation/AudioToolbox/Output.m, translation/CoreAudio/Hardware.m); with
UIBackgroundModes "audio" iOS keeps such an app running in the background while it plays, so
the game and its sessions are there when the player comes back. Both Info.plists get it, as in
pocket/patches/0013. Upstream candidate: brand-free, for any game with sound.

--- a/launcher/Info.plist
+++ b/launcher/Info.plist
@@ -6,6 +6,10 @@
 	<true/>
 	<key>CADisableMinimumFrameDurationOnPhone</key>
 	<true/>
+	<key>UIBackgroundModes</key>
+	<array>
+		<string>audio</string>
+	</array>
 	<key>CFBundleVersion</key>
 	<string>$(CURRENT_PROJECT_VERSION)</string>
 	<key>CFBundleShortVersionString</key>
--- a/authorization/App/Integrated-Info.plist
+++ b/authorization/App/Integrated-Info.plist
@@ -6,6 +6,10 @@
 	<true/>
 	<key>CADisableMinimumFrameDurationOnPhone</key>
 	<true/>
+	<key>UIBackgroundModes</key>
+	<array>
+		<string>audio</string>
+	</array>
 	<key>CFBundleShortVersionString</key>
 	<string>$(MARKETING_VERSION)</string>
 	<key>CFBundleVersion</key>
